1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,501
This video shows you how to review what the agent built: first its route, then its pipeline, over the records it captured. Then you deploy it.

2
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:18,771
The agent's work is on its own branch, fortigate-ecs. Open it.

3
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:28,880
Under Build, Routes shows the new route: fortigate-syslog, through FortiGate traffic and UTM to ECS, to elasticsearch.

4
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:45,510
Open it to see each part: the source, the pipeline, the destination, and its exceptions, left at their defaults.

5
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:59,193
Route a sample sends saved records through the route, without deploying anything. Choose the sample the agent saved, and click Run.

6
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:07,936
All twenty records go to elasticsearch. The Exceptions column is empty.

7
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:17,774
Sent shows each document as it would be sent to elasticsearch.

8
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:26,250
Now the pipeline. Open it from Pipelines.

9
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:42,390
Open each step to see how it was built. The first parser uses a grok pattern: it takes the syslog priority, and keeps the rest as fortigate.raw.

10
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:52,750
The second parser reads fortigate.raw as key value pairs: pairs are separated by a space, and each key from its value by an equals sign.

11
00:01:52,800 --> 00:02:02,028
It declares a type for each field it uses: integers for numbers such as ports, bytes and packets, and strings for the rest.

12
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:09,920
The eval step makes the Elastic Common Schema fields.

13
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:16,961
Each assignment names a field on the left, and where its value comes from on the right.

14
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:29,131
Choose the same sample, to see what each record becomes.

15
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:40,770
The first record is open. It has an @timestamp, and destination and event fields in Elastic Common Schema.

16
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:51,464
The original text is still there too, as _raw. Whether to keep it is your call, and you can ask the agent to change it.

17
00:02:54,280 --> 00:03:02,707
Scroll down the record to see the rest of what the agent mapped: network, observer, rule and source fields.

18
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:09,660
When what the agent built looks right, click Open for review.

19
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:21,875
The title and description are filled in from what changed. Click Open for review.

20
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:30,454
The change lists everything added: the route, and the pipeline with its three steps.

21
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:41,737
Click Approve and deploy, then confirm with Approve and deploy.

22
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:48,630
The Status page shows the change deploying to the worker, then Deployed.

23
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:55,310
Within a minute, rows go out: the out figure is the rows per second delivered to elasticsearch.

24
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:57,370
Check on learning.

25
00:03:57,570 --> 00:04:01,219
Where do you try a route without deploying it?

26
00:04:05,619 --> 00:04:08,938
Route a sample, on the Routes page.

27
00:04:09,838 --> 00:04:13,341
How do you see what one record becomes?

28
00:04:17,741 --> 00:04:22,371
Open the pipeline, choose a sample, and look at a record.

29
00:04:23,271 --> 00:04:28,291
The agent has built a pipeline on its branch. Is it running yet?

30
00:04:32,691 --> 00:04:39,106
No. It runs only after you open it for review, approve it, and deploy it.

31
00:04:40,006 --> 00:04:43,610
Next, you ask the agent about your fleet.
